# Gold rhyme annotation — Poe, "The Raven" (1845), stanza 1.
# Annotator: claude (draft; written from the verse text, not scanner output)

verse:
  Once upon a midnight dreary, while I pondered, weak and weary,
  Over many a quaint and curious volume of forgotten lore,
  While I nodded, nearly napping, suddenly there came a tapping,
  As of some one gently rapping, rapping at my chamber door.
  'Tis some visitor, I muttered, tapping at my chamber door,
  Only this and nothing more.

# the -eary family; "nearly" is Poe's own echo of it
group: dreary@0.4 weary@0.10 nearly@2.3

# the -apping family. Two same-word repetitions are vetoed below:
# repetition is rime riche, not rhyme (rapping,rapping / tapping...tapping)
group: napping@2.4 tapping@2.9 rapping@3.5 rapping@3.6 tapping@4.5
not: rapping@3.5 rapping@3.6
not: tapping@2.9 tapping@4.5

# the refrain family; door~door repetition vetoed
group: lore@1.9 door@3.10 door@4.9 more@5.4
not: door@3.10 door@4.9

# UNCERTAIN: "while I pondered" / "While I nodded" — the parallel frame
# makes pondered~nodded land for me; "forgotten" echoes them more faintly.
# Cut whatever your ear rejects.
group: pondered@0.7 nodded@2.2 forgotten@1.8
